<BaseLayout title="About" description="Why Regulatory Readiness focuses on the preparation layer: evidence, applicability, provenance, gaps, and change.">
  <header class="page-header"><div class="shell"><p class="eyebrow">About the thesis</p><h1>Publication is getting easier. Preparation is still fragmented.</h1><p class="lead">Organizations do not need another badge. They need a defensible way to gather facts, preserve sources, resolve disagreement, and show what remains undone.</p><Limitation /></div></header>
  <section class="section"><div class="shell split"><div><p class="eyebrow">Why this exists</p><h2>The underserved layer comes before the filing.</h2></div><div class="prose"><p>Generic repositories store documents. Point tools interpret one domain. Enterprise platforms can be too heavy for a small, time-pressed team. Advisers bring judgment, but often inherit scattered inputs.</p><p>Regulatory Readiness is designed to connect those pieces: requirements become an owned evidence plan, sources remain distinct from claims, and a reviewer receives a portable record of both answers and gaps.</p><div class="todo">TODO: owner to supply approved founder biography, legal entity, and public brand name after clearance.</div></div></div></section>
  <section class="section section-alt"><div class="shell"><div class="section-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow">Operating principles</p><h2>Honesty is part of the product model.</h2></div></div><div class="card-grid"><article class="card"><h3>Never hide uncertainty</h3><p>Unknown, conditional, disputed, missing, and stale are useful states—not copy problems.</p></article><article class="card"><h3>Keep the original distinct</h3><p>Evidence, extracted assertions, approved claims, and decisions serve different purposes.</p></article><article class="card"><h3>Govern the source</h3><p>Production pack snapshots require two-person review and recorded source metadata.</p></article></div></div></section>
  <CTA title="See whether the preparation layer fits your problem." />
